Rephrased Feedback
Connectivity Limitation
The SDET Buddy tool currently does not work when connected from the office or via VPN.
It requires direct access through the office network, which reduces usability.
IDE Support
At present, the tool only integrates with IntelliJ.
Support for more widely used IDEs such as VS Code is missing and should be considered.
Background & Reusability
Common setup steps like header configuration, token generation, UUID, and timestamp should always be placed in the Background.
These are currently repeated inside each scenario, reducing maintainability.
Payload Handling
Too many inline set payload statements make scenarios verbose and harder to read.
Payloads should be maintained in external JSON files, with only the necessary fields overridden in test cases.
Examples Table Usage
Example tables are often bulky and include the entire payload.
Instead, they should only contain the input variations relevant to the scenario.
Negative Scenario Generation
Negative test cases are being generated with “hallucinated” or invalid data because the user does not provide expected negative responses.
This requires manual cleanup and modification by the team.
Scalability Concerns
The current approach is not scalable:
For each API, the user must create a new prompt.
For each test case, a new project is generated with a separate Karate config file.
This creates overhead when testing multiple APIs within the same feature.
A more scalable solution is needed where multiple APIs can be covered in a single project/config without heavy setup per test.
